outputs.summary:
Generated land and War Sails routes are proven on runtime geometry in both seed worlds (accepted run generated_campaign-2248; independent re-review found no blocking defects).

Implementation:
- Diagnostics/GeneratedNavigationProbe.cs runs inside generated_campaign when navigationProbe=1. It covers:
  - the native route matrix: GetPathBetweenAIFaces and GetPathDistanceBetweenAIFaces for Default, Naval and All, in both directions;
  - face identities and the distance model compared with engine distances;
  - a seed-derived wall toggled with SetAbilityOfFacesWithId;
  - real party movement for the player and the AI, sampled every 0.1 s.
- tools/scenebin/build_bootstrap_world.py stamps the fixtures into the generated navmesh:
  - a wall (face group 5) at least 2 cells thick, accepted only when every shortest land route crosses it and a detour at least 4 cells longer exists;
  - a land-enclosed 3x3 lake (group 8);
  - a water-separated off-shore landmass of at least 9 cells.
- tools/summarize_navigation_probe.py builds evidence/runtime/navigation-probe.json from the raw results.

Engine finding: Scene.SetAbilityOfFacesWithId renumbers navmesh islands. CampaignVec2 caches its face, and MobileParty only moves when the current and next faces share an island. Every party that cached a face before a toggle therefore stops moving. This was the earlier 'AI stuck at spawn'. The probe now runs the AI trip before any toggle and refreshes party CampaignVec2 caches after each toggle; this is disclosed, and a follow-up was raised.

Also: Diagnostics/RuntimeNavigationProbe.cs (the 'nav' autorun) was accidentally overwritten in this session. It was reconstructed from the deployed DLL's IL, and the rebuilt method matches it instruction for instruction (455 lines after normalising compiler-generated names).

Not done:
- bridges (none authored);
- stitching (one navmesh per world);
- a regenerated-seed obstruction pair (the wall is toggled at runtime);
- the player sailing back;
- AI choosing ships on its own (the order is scripted);
- 4 code nits from the review, listed in reviewNotes.

Bounded streaming reader for FWGC++ split worlds (`fwg generate --split`), built in the worldbridge.

**Tokenizer: `worldbridge::json::Stream`** (pull, 1 MiB buffer)
- Every refusal gives an exact file, byte, line, column and JSONPath.
- Syntax errors point at the offending byte, and multi-byte errors at the first byte of the sequence.
- Rejects duplicate keys.
- Bounded by a 1 MiB token limit, depth 64, and 16 MiB in total for the keys of all open objects.

**Reader: `worldbridge::source::read_source`**
- One pass over world.json:
  - builds the top-level member index;
  - validates the contract (`worldgen/contracts/source-compatibility.json`);
  - validates all 166 layers while streaming: n×n, finite (null and 1e999 refused), seam and pole within tolerance, integral for categorical layers;
  - extracts bounded records (settlements.sites, humans.cores/hamlets/fortresses, key_locations.sites, the four `*_plans.files` indexes) under a retained-memory budget of 16 MiB.
- Every version is range-checked on the full 64-bit value.
- generator_version is descriptive text. Unknown optional blocks are skipped.
- Required record arrays must be present. A failed status in a contract block is refused.
- Joins:
  - each core joins to its city by site_id;
  - every hamlet and garrisoned fort is listed exactly once, by its own core;
  - a fort with no garrison uses the writer's sentinel (-1, "");
  - every record has exactly one plan entry.
- Plan files and the scene are reached only through the index paths:
  - FWGC++ character set only, inside the root, no aliases (case, 8.3, trailing dots, devices), no junction escapes, no hard links;
  - each file is opened and its identity, version and cross-references are checked.
- Hamlets dispatch on their actual fields, all or none, because harbour roles vary (including "landing"). Ordinal ids and stable uids are both kept.
- `read_layer` materializes one layer on request by seeking.
- seeds.layout stays a canonical u64 decimal string. `write_identity`/`read_identity` implement the strict `mnbl.source-identity` v1 contract (`worldgen/contracts/source-identity.schema.json`).
- CLI: `worldbridge --read-source DIR --contract FILE [--layers a,b] [--report F] [--identity F]`.

**Measured on the pinned producer's real exports** (seed 42; root sizes 83 MB, 199 MB, 601 MB and 2.07 GB; 796–920 plan files):
- 129: 2.0 s, peak working set 10.9 MB;
- 257: 3.5 s, 10.8 MB;
- 513: 9.1 s, 11.1 MB;
- 1025: 30.6 s, 11.0 MB;
- with one 1025² layer materialized: 17.7 MB;
- retained records: 0.74–0.85 MB.

Three crafted memory attacks inside the byte contract are refused with limit_exceeded at a sampled peak of 27–30 MB.

Tests: `source_reader_tests` (120 checks, CTest `source_contract`). Evidence: `tools/Test-SourceReader.ps1` writes `evidence/fwg/source-reader.json` (passed=true).

Not done: the old `worldbridge --source` path (MMR-12/16 heightfield export) still uses its earlier validator; switching it to the new reader belongs to the consumer tickets.
